# airgenome .convergence — 수렴 attr 기록
# format: single-file, 엔트리 단위로 @state 로 트래킹. JSON/JSONL/xattr 금지.

[cl-no-bypass-airgenome-only]
@state: converged
@date: 2026-04-20
@scope: bin/claudx + user/project .claude/settings.json (17 files) + modules/fs_write.hexa + run.hexa
@problem: Every cl-launched session ran with --dangerously-skip-permissions (auto-injected by claudx) + defaultMode: bypassPermissions (both user-level and 16 project-level settings.json). Net effect: Claude had a blanket prompt-free license across all work. The user wanted a stricter baseline where prompts are the normal path, and routine high-frequency operations (that would otherwise prompt) are covered by airgenome subcommands instead.
@decision: Make bypass opt-in across all cl sessions. Provide airgenome fs-write as the Bash-invoked escape hatch for sensitive paths (.claude/hooks/**, .claude/agents/**, .claude/commands/** — Claude Code's hardcoded sensitive-path prompt bypasses even when Write is in the allow list).
@changes:
  - bin/claudx: flipped default. Was "inject --dangerously-skip-permissions unless CLAUDX_SAFE=1". Now "inject only when CLAUDX_BYPASS=1". Legacy CLAUDX_SAFE env is ignored (still opt-out of a behavior that no longer runs by default).
  - .claude/settings.json × 17: removed .permissions.defaultMode when it was "bypassPermissions". Backups saved as *.bak.20260420-bypass-remove. One file (/mac_home/dev/hexa-lang/.claude/settings.json) deferred due to a mount-side permission error from the Linux VM; fix via the Mac bridge when it resettles.
  - modules/fs_write.hexa (new): Bash-invoked writer. Reads stdin (or positional arg), passes path through a hardcoded blacklist (empty, ".." traversal, /Users/ghost/.claude/{CLAUDE.md,hooks/**,agents/**,commands/**}), ensures parent dir, write_file(), appends audit to ~/.claude_mac_bridge/fs_write.jsonl.
  - run.hexa: dispatches "fs-write"/"fs_write" via run_module_args.
@why: Sensitive-path prompts are Claude Code's self-modification guard. The user wants that guard in force by default, with escape hatches routed through an owned, greppable surface (airgenome subcommands) instead of blanket bypass. Any escape hatch that lands in the repo becomes reviewable; any bypass env var that has to be set explicitly is visible.
@tradeoff: First few days will surface allow-list gaps — patterns Claude tries that aren't allowed will prompt. Fix loop: either add an allow rule to settings.json or add an airgenome subcommand. Longer-term: allow list converges on "Bash(airgenome *)" + a few primitives, and every prompt becomes a signal that we need a new subcommand.
@client-usage:
  - cat content | airgenome fs-write <path>
  - airgenome fs-write <path> "inline content"
  - CLAUDX_BYPASS=1 cl ...   # one-off emergency bypass
@deferred: /mac_home/dev/hexa-lang/.claude/settings.json bypassPermissions removal (see @changes). Backup not yet created; original untouched.
@rollback: revert commit; restore from .bak.20260420-bypass-remove files if settings need reverting; CLAUDX_BYPASS=1 cl reproduces old behavior case-by-case while observing the new default.

[mac-bridge-hexa-only]
@state: converged
@date: 2026-04-20
@scope: modules/mac_exec.hexa + modules/mac_bridge_worker.hexa + bin/mac_bridge_worker (shim) + Library/LaunchAgents/com.airgenome.mac-bridge.plist + run.hexa (mac-exec dispatch)
@problem: Claude Code runs inside a Linux VM; the Mac host is unreachable via SSH/network. Mac-only actions (launchctl, git push with stored credentials, brew, launch-agent reloads) were not executable from the VM, forcing the user to copy-paste commands into their terminal each time.
@decision: File-based bridge. Linux VM writes a request file under /mac_home/.claude_mac_bridge/req/<uuid>.cmd; a launchd agent on Mac (com.airgenome.mac-bridge, WatchPaths=~/.claude_mac_bridge/req) wakes and runs it; results land in rsp/<uuid>.{out,err,rc,done}. Client polls the .done marker, streams stdout/stderr, forwards exit code.
@why: No network needed — the /mac_home mount already provides a shared, low-latency transport. Launchd WatchPaths is idle-cheap (fires only on directory change) and auto-restarts across reboots. 5-minute per-command timeout (SIGTERM→SIGKILL) prevents runaway commands from pinning the watchdog slot.
@hexa-only: Original 2026-04-20 version was two shell scripts (bin/mac_exec, bin/mac_bridge_worker). Per user directive "hexa-lang only — 문서 말고 코드로", language logic was rewritten as .hexa modules. bin/mac_bridge_worker remains as a ~3-line bash shim (launchd ProgramArguments still references a bin/ path in the existing bootstrap; the shim execs the real Mac hexa at /Users/ghost/Dev/hexa-lang/hexa). Updated plist points to the hexa binary directly, so after a one-time launchctl reload the shim is bypassed and only .hexa carries logic.
@pitfalls:
  - /opt/homebrew/bin/hexa is a DIFFERENT binary ("HEXA-LANG — Perfect Number Language", a math REPL). The correct compiler is /Users/ghost/Dev/hexa-lang/hexa (Mach-O arm64). Do not route launchd or shims through /opt/homebrew/bin/hexa.
  - Linux VM's hexa (/usr/local/bin/hexa) is wrapped by safe_hexa_launchd.sh which calls macOS `launchctl` and fails on Linux. Set HEXA_NO_LAUNCHD=1 to bypass the wrapper when running .hexa modules from the VM.
  - macOS `ps` does not need `uid` to match the Mac uid value; the hexa client uses `env("HOME")` on Mac-side and the mount path on Linux-side.
@files:
  - modules/mac_exec.hexa — Linux VM → Mac client (atomic write_file → mv, .done polling with 200ms interval, stdout via read_file+print, stderr via shell fd2, rc via exit())
  - modules/mac_bridge_worker.hexa — Mac launchd worker (atomic mv claim, bash '<running-file>' with 5-min watchdog, writes rsp files, jsonl audit log)
  - bin/mac_bridge_worker — 3-line launchd shim (exec /Users/ghost/Dev/hexa-lang/hexa run modules/mac_bridge_worker.hexa)
  - Library/LaunchAgents/com.airgenome.mac-bridge.plist — WatchPaths + KeepAlive off + ThrottleInterval 1s; ProgramArguments points directly at hexa+module (shim becomes vestigial after plist reload)
  - run.hexa:1577-1600 — airgenome mac-exec dispatch with rc forwarding via /tmp/airgenome_macexec_rc_$$ file
@audit: ~/.claude_mac_bridge/log.jsonl appends {ts,id,rc,cmd_head} per request.
@client-usage: `HEXA_NO_LAUNCHD=1 hexa run modules/mac_exec.hexa "shell command"` (from VM). Exit code of the remote command is forwarded.
@rollback: revert commit; prior state was an equivalent pair of shell scripts bin/mac_exec + bin/mac_bridge_worker with the plist ProgramArguments pointing to the shell worker.

